      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "When the railroad reached Troutdale, the Trading Company doubled as a passenger and freight station, sparking a boom.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        3
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://www.newrivernotes.com/history-of-troutdale-virginia/",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "The United States Spruce Lumber Company built a large band‑mill at Fox Bottoms, becoming Troutdale’s first factory.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        9
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://www.newrivernotes.com/history-of-troutdale-virginia/",
      "category": "landmark",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "A new station erected around 1908 remained in use until about 1933, serving the town for over two decades.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        15
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://www.newrivernotes.com/history-of-troutdale-virginia/",
      "category": "landmark",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "text": "The original station, repurposed as a warehouse, was destroyed by fire in 1911.",
